"""Combined ankle and actual-foot experiment report, with fixed detection replay."""
import shutil,json,sys
from pathlib import Path
import feet_pilot as f
import ankle_pilot as a
import ankle_deliver as render

OUT=f.OUT

def report():
    feet=f.read(OUT/'accuracy.json');ankles=f.read(a.OUT/'accuracy.json');cfg=f.read(OUT/'settings.json');coverage=f.read(OUT/'coverage.json')
    control=f.read(OUT/'rtmw_ankle_control.json')
    methods={'Box bottom':feet['development_final']['current'],'YOLO ankles':ankles['development_final']['ankles'],'RTMW ankles (same-model control)':control['development_final'],'RTMW toes / heels':feet['development_final']['feet']}
    rows=''
    for label,d in methods.items():
        rows+=f"<tr><td>{label}</td><td>{d['raw']['correct_within_3pct']} / 120</td><td>{d['map']['floor_correct_within_3pct']} / 120</td><td>{d['raw']['matched_visible_anchors']}</td><td>{d['raw']['median_error_pct_width']:.2f}%</td></tr>"
    val=[feet['validation']['current'],ankles['validation']['ankles'],control['validation'],feet['validation']['feet']]
    valrows=''.join(f"<tr><td>{n}</td><td>{d['raw']['correct_within_3pct']} / 48</td><td>{d['map']['floor_correct_within_3pct']} / 48</td></tr>" for n,d in zip(methods,val))
    body=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Feet and ankle pilot</title><style>
body{{background:#101619;color:#edf1f4;font:17px/1.65 system-ui;margin:0}}main{{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:44px 24px 72px}}h1{{font-size:44px;line-height:1.12;margin:18px 0}}h2{{font-size:25px;margin-top:34px}}.tag{{color:#96d4c7;letter-spacing:2px;font-size:13px;text-transform:uppercase}}.card{{background:#1c282d;padding:24px;border-radius:14px;margin:24px 0}}video,img{{width:100%;border-radius:10px}}button{{padding:12px 16px;background:#293e47;color:#edf1f4;border:1px solid #526d79;border-radius:8px;margin:0 8px 12px 0;cursor:pointer}}a{{color:#9fcefa}}.muted{{color:#acbbc3}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:15px}}td,th{{text-align:left;padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4d55}}th{{color:#96d4c7}}p{{max-width:940px}}</style><main>
<div class="tag">Dance project · floor-input pilot · same 12-second clip</div><h1>Actual foot landmarks help,<br>but contact is still unresolved.</h1><p>We tested pretrained ankle detection, then a whole-body model with toes and heels. The foot model performs much better than directly projecting ankle joints. Neither beats the current box-bottom map overall on our reviewed references. No new training labels or retraining were used.</p>
<div class="card"><button onclick="play('feet_comparison.mp4')">Box bottom vs toes/heels</button><button onclick="play('ankle_only_comparison.mp4')">Box bottom vs ankles</button><button onclick="play('movement_half_speed.mp4')">Movement · half speed</button><button onclick="play('floor_work_half_speed.mp4')">Floor work · half speed</button><video id="video" src="feet_comparison.mp4" poster="comparison_195.jpg" controls playsinline preload="metadata"></video><p class="muted">Left: the current map. Right: the chosen pose rule. Yellow dots are ankles, green dots are toes/heels, blue squares are knees. A white cross on the dancer marks the selected proposal. Solid map dots are filtered proxies; hollow dots are predictions for up to one second. Unknown contacts remain unknown.</p></div>
<h2>What stayed fixed</h2><p>The existing YOLO detections, all dancer IDs, calibration, median smoothing and bounded map filter are unchanged. The comparison replaces the point going into the map. We did not use the failed Kalman filter, alter tracking, or silently fall back to box bottoms.</p>
<h2>What changed</h2><p>First, YOLO26m pose estimated ankle joints using scene tiles and enlarged dancer crops. Its 17-joint skeleton does not include heels or toes. Second, RTMW estimated whole-body landmarks directly inside the same frozen YOLO boxes. We used its toe and heel landmarks, retained the lower support foot when the other was clearly raised, and tested a separate knee-support rule for kneeling.</p><p>We also checked RTMW ankles using the exact same pose output, to separate the landmark choice from a model change: filtered correct points rose from {control['development_final']['map']['floor_correct_within_3pct']} with RTMW ankles to {feet['development_final']['feet']['map']['floor_correct_within_3pct']} with its toes/heels.</p><p>The selected foot rule is <strong>{cfg['mode']}</strong>, confidence threshold {cfg['confidence']}. Model confidence is not a probability of touching the floor. The knee heuristic was tested too; it rarely activated and did not improve the final-section count.</p>
<h2>Final floor-work section: eight reviewed frames</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Raw correct</th><th>Correct after same smoothing</th><th>Visible references with raw estimate</th><th>Median raw error</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table><p class="muted">There are 120 visible support references. Correct means within 3% of mat width. Missing outputs count against the correct/all-reference score. Raw error is calculated only where an estimate exists, so it must be read together with coverage. Filtered outputs include explicitly marked short predictions.</p>
<h2>Standing and raised-leg section: four validation frames</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Raw correct</th><th>Correct after same smoothing</th></tr></thead><tbody>{valrows}</tbody></table><p class="muted">Each model's rule and confidence were chosen using raw-point joint F1 on these validation frames. Six options were considered per model. This is iterative development on an already reviewed clip, not an untouched test set.</p>
<h2>Inspect the actual foot points</h2><img src="pose_detail_195.jpg" alt="RTMW ankle, toe and heel landmarks on standing dancers"><p>The toe/heel points reach closer to the mat than ankle joints. A raised foot must still be excluded. In overlaps, a top-down pose model can follow the wrong body within a box even though the box and ID are unchanged.</p><img src="pose_detail_285.jpg" alt="RTMW landmarks on kneeling dancers"><p>During floor work, feet may be behind the knees or hidden. An accurate toe coordinate is not necessarily the dancer's desired stage position. Our existing references sometimes use the center between supporting knees, so ankle and foot points can be measuring a different target.</p>
<h2>Coverage and identity</h2><p>Across 6,000 dancer-frame records, the current map has {coverage['current'].get('proxy',0)} solid proxies, {coverage['current'].get('estimated',0)} predictions and {coverage['current'].get('unknown',0)} unknown floor locations. The foot map has {coverage['feet'].get('proxy',0)} solid proxies, {coverage['feet'].get('estimated',0)} predictions and {coverage['feet'].get('unknown',0)} unknown locations. All methods retain the same 88 correct original-ID matches out of 145 reviewed visible boxes. This experiment cannot repair those identity errors.</p>
<div class="card"><strong>Decision: do not switch the whole map to ankles or feet yet.</strong><p>Keep the current map as the baseline. Toe/heel detection is a more plausible candidate than ankle projection, but it is not a complete contact estimator. Before requesting more labeling, agree on one target: the center of visible supporting feet when standing, the chosen support location during floor work, and an explicit unknown state when contact cannot be seen. Independent review of a small sample is necessary because the current labels are approximate.</p></div>
<h2>What this experiment does and does not establish</h2><p>It tests two pretrained model pipelines without new training data. It demonstrates why a reliable-looking joint can still be the wrong point for floor projection. It does not prove that a model trained for this choreography could not improve. The existing annotations are assistant-reviewed, the mat calibration is approximate, and neither model classifies actual ground contact.</p>
<p><a href="Feet_and_Ankles_Pilot.ipynb">Concise notebook</a> · <a href="accuracy.json">Foot scores</a> · <a href="ankle_accuracy.json">Ankle scores</a> · <a href="selection.json">Foot validation settings</a> · <a href="all_final_diagnostics.json">All foot-rule diagnostics</a> · <a href="provenance.json">Frozen-input verification</a></p>
<p class="muted">Sources: <a href="https://docs.ultralytics.com/tasks/pose/">YOLO pose keypoints</a>, <a href="https://github.com/open-mmlab/mmpose/blob/main/configs/_base_/datasets/coco_wholebody.py">COCO-WholeBody toe/heel definitions</a>, <a href="https://github.com/Tau-J/rtmlib">RTMLib inference</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.08634">RTMW paper</a>.</p>
</main><script>function play(src){{const v=document.getElementById('video');v.src=src;v.load();v.play();}}</script></html>'''
    (OUT/'START_HERE.html').write_text(body,encoding='utf-8')
    shutil.copy2(a.OUT/'ankle_comparison.mp4',OUT/'ankle_only_comparison.mp4')
    shutil.copy2(a.OUT/'accuracy.json',OUT/'ankle_accuracy.json')
    shutil.copy2(a.OUT/'selection.json',OUT/'ankle_selection.json')
    import nbformat as nbf
    nb=nbf.v4.new_notebook();nb.metadata={'kernelspec':{'display_name':'Python 3','language':'python','name':'python3'}}
    nb.cells=[nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('# Feet and ankles as floor-position input\n\nWe kept YOLO boxes, dancer IDs, calibration and smoothing fixed. We tested pretrained YOLO ankles and RTMW toes/heels. No extra labeling or retraining.'),nbf.v4.new_code_cell("import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom IPython.display import Video, Image, display\nroot=Path.cwd()\nload=lambda n: json.loads((root/n).read_text())\nload('settings.json'),load('provenance.json')"),nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('## Why test toes and heels?\n\nAn ankle joint is above the ground. YOLO COCO17 does not include toe/heel landmarks. RTMW whole-body pose does; it runs directly on each frozen detected box. We test which foot is plausibly supporting the dancer, but this remains a heuristic.'),nbf.v4.new_code_cell("display(Image(filename=str(root/'pose_detail_195.jpg')))\ndisplay(Video(str(root/'feet_comparison.mp4'),embed=False,width=1000))"),nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('## Compare accuracy and coverage\n\nThe final section contains 120 visible support references over eight frames. Report correct positions against all references, including missed estimates. Read median errors alongside coverage. These references are approximate and already reviewed.'),nbf.v4.new_code_cell("foot=load('accuracy.json')['development_final']\nankle=load('ankle_accuracy.json')['development_final']['ankles']\nfor name,v in [('box bottom',foot['current']),('ankles',ankle),('toes/heels',foot['feet'])]:\n    print(name,'raw correct:',v['raw']['correct_within_3pct'],'filtered correct:',v['map']['floor_correct_within_3pct'],'/120')\nload('coverage.json')"),nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('## Floor work changes the target\n\nFeet can lie behind supporting knees. A correctly detected toe is not necessarily the desired stage position. A knee rule was tested, but rarely activated and did not improve the final count. Hidden contact must remain unknown rather than becoming a fabricated coordinate.'),nbf.v4.new_code_cell("display(Image(filename=str(root/'pose_detail_285.jpg')))"),nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('## Decision\n\nToe/heel points improved over ankle projection but did not beat the current box-bottom map overall. Keep the current baseline and defer broad labeling. The next requirement is a consistent support-point definition plus visibility, with independent review of a small reference sample. This is not evidence that more ankle labels alone will fix the project.\n\nReproduce from the DL project root with the existing environment: `ankle_pilot.py infer`, `ankle_pilot.py`, `ankle_deliver.py`, then `feet_pilot.py infer`, `feet_pilot.py`, `feet_deliver.py` (scripts in `Dance_Floor_Contact`). Existing source video, frozen tracks and calibration are required. The environment details are in `environment.json`.\n\n[YOLO pose](https://docs.ultralytics.com/tasks/pose/) · [RTMW](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.08634)')]
    from nbclient import NotebookClient
    NotebookClient(nb,timeout=120,kernel_name='python3',resources={'metadata':{'path':str(OUT)}}).execute();nbf.write(nb,OUT/'Feet_and_Ankles_Pilot.ipynb')
    import importlib.metadata as meta
    f.write('environment.json',{'python':sys.version,'packages':{p:meta.version(p) for p in ['torch','ultralytics','numpy','opencv-python','opencv-contrib-python','rtmlib','onnxruntime-gpu']},'runtime_note':'ONNX Runtime GPU1.26.0 uses CUDA12-compatible libraries loaded from the existing PyTorch installation. RTMLib uses this GPU package in place of its default CPU onnxruntime dependency.'})
    for name in ['ankle_pilot.py','ankle_deliver.py','feet_pilot.py','feet_deliver.py']:shutil.copy2(Path(__file__).parent/name,OUT/name)

if __name__=='__main__':
    data=f.read(OUT/'tracks.json');labels=f.read(f.LABEL_DIR/'labels.json');cfg=f.read(OUT/'settings.json')
    render.OUT=OUT;render.write=f.write
    render.render(data,labels,cfg,prefix='feet');report();render.verify(data,prefix='feet')
